---
/**
 * The shared-support figure. This is the site's first content figure, so it
 * also sets the diagram convention, following website/cfd's ForkTree:
 * hand-drawn inline SVG in the instrument vocabulary, hairline strokes and
 * accent nodes, animated through the shared `[data-anim-draw]` contract rather
 * than any per-diagram script.
 *
 * Geometry only in the SVG, labels in HTML. SVG text scales with the viewBox,
 * so a label legible on a desktop lands at eight or nine pixels on a phone.
 *
 * Three legs, not eight. Three is the smallest number that shows a shared leg
 * and an unshared one at the same time, which is the whole content of the
 * figure: the check fires on the middle rail and skips the outer two.
 *
 * `vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke` is deliberately absent. It resolves
 * `stroke-dasharray` in device pixels, which fights the `pathLength="100"`
 * normalization the draw-in depends on and renders every stroke dashed.
 */
---
<figure class="supports">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 100 46"
    preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
    data-anim-draw
    role="img"
    aria-label="Three horizontal Hilbert legs. One factor spans the top two legs, a second factor spans the bottom two. They meet on the middle leg, which is drawn in the accent colour, and that shared leg is where the commutator has to vanish."
  >
    <!-- Three Hilbert legs. The middle one is shared, so it is the accent. -->
    <line x1="6" y1="10" x2="94" y2="10" pathLength="100" />
    <line x1="6" y1="23" x2="94" y2="23" pathLength="100" class="shared" />
    <line x1="6" y1="36" x2="94" y2="36" pathLength="100" />

    <!-- Factor A: a bracket over legs 0 and 1. -->
    <path d="M20,4 L44,4 L44,29 L20,29 Z" pathLength="100" class="factor" />
    <!-- Factor B: a bracket over legs 1 and 2. -->
    <path d="M56,17 L80,17 L80,42 L56,42 Z" pathLength="100" class="factor" />

    <!-- Nodes, in stagger order: A on its two legs, the shared crossing, then
         B on its two legs. -->
    <circle cx="32" cy="10" r="1.3" />
    <circle cx="32" cy="23" r="1.3" class="on-shared" />
    <circle cx="50" cy="23" r="1.7" class="meet" />
    <circle cx="68" cy="23" r="1.3" class="on-shared" />
    <circle cx="68" cy="36" r="1.3" />
  </svg>

  <div class="s-labels" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="s-l">Factor ρ_A</span>
    <span class="s-l">Shared leg</span>
    <span class="s-l">Factor ρ_B</span>
    <span class="s-l">‖[ρ_A, ρ_B]‖_F ≤ tol</span>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    Supports that do not intersect impose no obligation and are skipped. Where
    they do, both factors are embedded onto the union of their legs, the
    commutator is formed there, and its Frobenius norm is compared against a
    forward-error threshold.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .supports { margin: var(--space-5) 0; }

  .supports svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 44rem;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* Stroke and fill are scoped per diagram; global.css carries only the dash
     and node timing, never the colour. Stroke width is in user units; at the
     44rem cap this is about one device pixel, the hairline the site uses. */
  .supports line,
  .supports path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--line-2);
    stroke-width: 0.16;
  }
  .supports line.shared { stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 0.24; }
  .supports path.factor { stroke: var(--fg-2); stroke-width: 0.2; }

  .supports circle { fill: var(--fg-2); }
  .supports circle.on-shared,
  .supports circle.meet { fill: var(--accent); }

  .s-labels {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .s-labels { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  }
  .s-l {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--fg-1);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
